<article class="env-lemma" id="09NK">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/09NK">Lemma <span data-tag="09NK">33.42.5</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a ring, $I \subset A$ an ideal, $\mathfrak p_1, \ldots , \mathfrak p_ r$ primes of $A$, and $\overline{f} \in A/I$ an element. If $I \not\subset \mathfrak p_ i$ for all $i$, then there exists an $f \in A$, $f \not\in \mathfrak p_ i$ which maps to $\overline{f}$ in $A/I$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We may assume there are no inclusion relations among the $\mathfrak p_ i$ (by removing the smaller primes). First pick any $f \in A$ lifting $\overline{f}$. Let $S$ be the set $s \in \{ 1, \ldots , r\} $ such that $f \in \mathfrak p_ s$. If $S$ is empty we are done. If not, consider the ideal $J = I \prod _{i \not\in S} \mathfrak p_ i$. Note that $J$ is not contained in $\mathfrak p_ s$ for $s \in S$ because there are no inclusions among the $\mathfrak p_ i$ and because $I$ is not contained in any $\mathfrak p_ i$. Hence we can choose $g \in J$, $g \not\in \mathfrak p_ s$ for $s \in S$ by Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00DS" data-tag="00DS">10.15.2</a>. Then $f + g$ is a solution to the problem posed by the lemma. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>